\code{log(mu)}; the second models \code{log(k)}. A second formula of \code{~ 1} estimates
constant shape. The conventional Weibull scale is
\code{lambda = mu / Gamma(1 + 1/k)}. See \code{\link[=weibull]{weibull()}} for the distribution and
conditional interpretation of the mean. Both predictors accept offsets.
Weights multiply the exact uncensored likelihood. Zero weights are allowed.
The two predictors require separate coefficient blocks.

In \code{gam()}, REML supports outer Newton, outer BFGS, and extended
Fellner--Schall (EFS). EFS requires the smoothing parameters to be all
free and unlinked, or all fixed. NCV and QNCV use outer BFGS, with
leave-one-out or explicit \code{nei} neighbourhoods. The criterion uses
one-step deletion predictions. QNCV also approximates held-out likelihood
contributions quadratically. Use explicit \code{nei} with \code{jackknife = FALSE}
for fully unpenalized NCV/QNCV models on mgcv 1.9-4.
The general-family \code{nlm} and \code{optim} branches of mgcv 1.9-4 and 1.9-5
require a null-model objective scale that mgcv leaves unset.

In \code{scasm()}, REML uses EFS. mgcv 1.9-4 and 1.9-5 expose no
\code{method}, \code{optimizer} or \code{nei} arguments in \code{scasm()}, so NCV and QNCV
